            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-form"} differential 1-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            but 2-form gauge potentials (“nonabelian
            = succeed "\u201D)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-fields"} B-fields
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-connections"} principal 2-connections
          %p
            Specifically, in (
            %a{:href => "#BanksSeiberg08"}> Banks-Seiberg 08, (2.4)-(2.9)
            ) is an argument saying that some properties of the low energy limit of abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            in four dimensions with (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"}> Higgs-like
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            to a discrete gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            are described by a dual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BF-theory"} BF-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
